# Hosting example; npm start does not load this file automatically.
HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=4173
# Set an absolute writable persistent directory in your hosting environment.
# COMMUNITY_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/el-nino-watch
# Optional off-site backups of reports to a PRIVATE Bunny Storage zone (no pull zone attached).
# BACKUP_STORAGE_ZONE=
# BACKUP_STORAGE_KEY=
# BACKUP_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=https://storage.bunnycdn.com
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS=6
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# BACKUP_RESTORE_IF_EMPTY=true
# Optional: ReliefWeb (UN OCHA) reports in the El Niño news flash. Request a free appname at
# https://apidoc.reliefweb.int/parameters#appname; the API refuses names it has not approved.
# RELIEFWEB_APPNAME=
# Optional: Google Flood Hub river forecasts in Alerts. Needs a Google Cloud project Google has approved
# for the Flood Forecasting API (https://developers.google.com/flood-forecasting) and an API key with that
# API enabled. Without it the source is switched off.
# GOOGLE_FLOOD_API_KEY=
# Optional: a paid Open-Meteo plan (https://open-meteo.com/en/pricing). The free API is for non-commercial use
# only; set this before the site is used commercially. With it, weather, river and elevation requests use
# Open-Meteo's customer hosts. Never commit or print it.
# OPEN_METEO_API_KEY=
# Optional: review keys for people who approve new community reports at /review, comma-separated, each at
# least 24 random characters. The owner's STATS_KEY also opens the queue. New reports wait there either way.
# Give each reviewer the link https://<site>/review#key=<their key>. Remove a key to take a reviewer off.
# REVIEW_KEYS=
# Optional: WhatsApp alerts through Meta's WhatsApp Business Platform (DEPLOYMENT.md, "WhatsApp alerts").
# All five of the first settings are needed; without them the feature is off.
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# WHATSAPP_NUMBER=
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE=flood_alert
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_SO_LANGUAGE=
